.TH SH-PLOT 1
.SH NAME
plot \- shell script interface to plot(2)'s graph command language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load plot

.B id := ${plot new
.I WxH
.RB [ title ]
.B }
.br
.B plot
.I id
.I graph-command...
.br
.B plot
.I id
.B draw
.br
.B ${plot
.I id
.B rows
.I name
.B }
.br
.B ${plot
.I id
.B value
.I name row column
.B }
.br
.B ${plot
.I id
.B invert
.I view x y
.B }
.br
.B plot del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Plot
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR plot (2)'s
own graph command language
.RB ( table / colour / view / content / split / axis / line / scale /
.B clear
and any table's own
.BR append ,
documented in full in
.IR plot (2)),
dispatched the same way
.IR sh-tk (1)
dispatches raw tk commands: a handle id directly followed by the
command text, no keyword of its own in between.
.IR Plot (2)
itself is display-agnostic \(em
.B Plotter.new()
just takes an image and a font, and draws into whatever image it's
handed \(em so the one thing this module adds beyond forwarding to
.BR Plotter.cmd()/rows()/value()/invert()
is a window to draw
.I into:
a handle here is a small
.B Toplevel
(opened the same way
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
opens its own \(em a
.B panel
widget inside its own frame, packed to fill; an offscreen buffer image
the same size, bound to it via
.BR tk->putimage ())
plus the
.B Plotter
itself, over that buffer. This is independent of
.IR sh-tk (1)'s
own window handles \(em that module's own window table is private to
it, unreachable from here.
.PP
Only
.BR new / del
(handle lifecycle) and
.BR draw / rows / value / invert
(this module's own additions \(em not part of
.IR plot (2)'s
string vocabulary at all) are special-cased first words, laid over a
generic forward exactly the way
.IR sh-tk (1)'s
own
.BR window / winctl / onscreen / namechan / del
are laid over
.IR its
generic forward. Every other verb
.RB ( table / colour / view / content / split / axis / line / scale /
.BR clear ,
a table's own
.BR append ,
and anything
.IR plot (2)
adds later) is
.IR plot (2)'s
own language, forwarded verbatim; this file never recognizes any of
it, the same discipline
.IR sh-dynsys (1)
and
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
itself already hold to. A forwarded command's own failure is reported
as a soft
.B $status
(the same convention
.IR sh-tk (1)
uses for a bad raw tk command) rather than aborting the script; only a
bad handle id or a missing argument raises.
.PP
.B ${plot new
.I WxH
.RB [ title ]
.BR }
opens the window (a bare top-level
.B sh
gets a fresh draw context of its own; an
.B sh
already hosted inside one \(em under
.IR wm (1),
say \(em reuses it, the same ambiguity
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own
.B init()
resolves the identical way) and yields a handle.
.B Plot
.I id
.I graph-command...
joins the remaining words back into one line and hands it to the
handle's own
.BR Plotter.cmd() .
.B Plot
.I id
.B draw
calls the handle's own
.BR Plotter.draw() ,
flushes the buffer, and marks the panel dirty so the window actually
redraws \(em the same sequence
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own
.B redraw()
ends with.
.PP
.BR Rows ,
.BR value ,
and
.B invert
are
.IR plot (2)'s
own functions of the same name, unchanged.
.B Plot del
frees the handle \(em it does
.I not
close the window: unlike
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
(which calls
.B "sys->pctl(Sys->NEWPGRP, nil)"
before opening its own toplevel, so
.B "tkclient->wmctl(top, \(dqexit\(dq)"
only kills
.I its
own group), this module's handle lives inside whatever process is
running the calling
.B sh
script \(em the same
.B exit
call would kill that script outright.
.B Del
leaves the window orphaned on screen instead, the same trade-off
.IR sh-tk (1)'s
own
.B "tk del"
already makes for exactly this reason.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	load plot
	id := ${plot new 400x300 History}
	plot $id table history t y
	plot $id colour ink 16r4ec9b0ff
	plot $id content margin 10 10 10 10
	plot $id view v content
	plot $id scale v x 0 10
	plot $id scale v y 0 100
	plot $id history append 0.0 0.0
	plot $id history append 5.0 25.0
	plot $id history append 10.0 100.0
	plot $id axis v x time
	plot $id axis v y value
	plot $id line v history x t y y colour ink width 2
	plot $id draw
	echo rows: ${plot $id rows history}
	plot del $id
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/plot.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR plot (2),
.IR tk (2),
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-tk (1),
.IR sh-dynsys (1),
.IR sh-pde (1),
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
.SH BUGS
.B Plot
.I id
.B table
must not be reissued after any
.B append
\(em
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.B bind()
unconditionally replaces a table with a fresh, empty one, silently
discarding every appended row (see
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own BUGS-adjacent header comment for the incident this was first found
from). This module does nothing to protect a script from that; timing
which commands run once versus every redraw is the caller's own
business, the same as it is there.
